[]byte("<pre>Network Working Group                                          Vint Cerf\x0aRequest for Comments: 20                                            UCLA\x0a                                                        October 16, 1969\x0a\x0a\x0a                  <span class=\x22h1\x22>ASCII format for Network Interchange</span>\x0a\x0a   For concreteness, we suggest the use of standard 7-bit ASCII embedded\x0a   in an 8 bit byte whose high order bit is always 0.  This leads to the\x0a   standard code given on the attached page, copies from USAS X3, 4-\x0a   1968.  This code will be used over HOST-HOST primary connections.\x0a   Break characters will be defined by the receiving remote host, e.g.\x0a   SRI uses \x22.\x22 (ASCII X'2E' or 2/14) as the end-of-line character,\x0a   where as UCLA uses X'OD' or 0/13 (carriage return).\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0aUSA Standard Code for Information Interchange\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-1\x22 href=\x22#section-1\x22>1</a>. Scope</span>\x0a\x0a   This coded character set is to be used for the general interchange of\x0a   information among information processing systems, communication\x0a   systems, and associated equipment.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 1]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-2\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-2\x22 href=\x22#section-2\x22>2</a>. Standard Code</span>\x0a\x0a|----------------------------------------------------------------------|\x0a  B  \x5c b7 ------------&gt;| 0   | 0   | 0   | 0   | 1   | 1   | 1   | 1   |\x0a   I  \x5c  b6 ----------&gt;| 0   | 0   | 1   | 1   | 0   | 0   | 1   | 1   |\x0a    T  \x5c   b5 --------&gt;| 0   | 1   | 0   | 1   | 0   | 1   | 0   | 1   |\x0a     S                 |-----------------------------------------------|\x0a               COLUMN-&gt;| 0   | 1   | 2   | 3   | 4   | 5   | 6   | 7   |\x0a|b4 |b3 |b2 |b1 | ROW  |     |     |     |     |     |     |     |     |\x0a+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\x0a| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0    | NUL | DLE | SP  | 0   | @   | P   |   ` |   p |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1    | SOH | DC1 | !   | 1   | A   | Q   |   a |   q |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 2    | STX | DC2 | \x22   | 2   | B   | R   |   b |   r |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 3    | ETX | DC3 | #   | 3   | C   | S   |   c |   s |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 4    | EOT | DC4 | $   | 4   | D   | T   |  d  |   t |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 5    | ENQ | NAK | %   | 5   | E   | U   |  e  |   u |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 6    | ACK | SYN | &amp;   | 6   | F   | V   |  f  |   v |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 7    | BEL | ETB | '   | 7   | G   | W   |  g  |   w |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 8    | BS  | CAN | (   | 8   | H   | X   |  h  |   x |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 9    | HT  | EM  | )   | 9   | I   | Y   |  i  |   y |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 10   | LF  | SUB | *   | :   | J   | Z   |  j  |   z |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 11   | VT  | ESC | +   |  ;  | K   | [   |  k  |   { |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 12   | FF  | FS  | ,   | &lt;   | L   | \x5c   |  l  |   | |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 13   | CR  | GS  | -   | =   | M   | ]   |  m  |   } |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 14   | SO  | RS  | .   | &gt;   | N   | ^   |  n  |   ~ |\x0a|---|---|---|---|------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\x0a| 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 15   | SI  | US  | /   | ?   | O   | _   |  o  | DEL |\x0a+----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 2]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-3\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-3\x22 href=\x22#section-3\x22>3</a>. Character Representation and Code Identification</span>\x0a\x0a   The standard 7-bit character representation, with b7 the high-order\x0a   bit and b1 the low-order bit, is shown below:\x0a\x0a   EXAMPLE: The bit representation for the character \x22K,\x22 positioned in\x0a   column 4, row 11, is\x0a\x0a   b7 b6 b5 b4 b3 b2 b1\x0a   1  0  0  1  0  1  1\x0a\x0a\x0a   The code table position for the character \x22K\x22 may also be represented\x0a   by the notation \x22column 4, row 11\x22 or alternatively as \x224/11.\x22  The\x0a   decimal equivalent of the binary number formed by bits b7, b6, and\x0a   b5, collectively, forms the column number, and the decimal equivalent\x0a   of the binary number formed by bits b4, b3, b2, and b1, collectively,\x0a   forms the row number.\x0a\x0a   The standard code may be identified by the use of the notation ASCII\x0a   or USASCII.\x0a\x0a   The notation ASCII (pronounced as'-key) or USASCII (pronounced you-\x0a   sas'-key) should ordinarily be taken to mean the code prescribed by\x0a   the latest issue of the standard.  To explicitly designate a\x0a   particular (perhaps prior) issue, the last two digits of the year of\x0a   issue may be appended, as, \x22ASCII 63\x22 or \x22USASCII 63\x22.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 3]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-4\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-4\x22 href=\x22#section-4\x22>4</a>. Legend</span>\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h3\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-4.1\x22 href=\x22#section-4.1\x22>4.1</a> Control Characters</span>\x0a\x0a   NUL Null                                DLE Data Link Escape (CC)\x0a\x0a   SOH Start of Heading (CC)               DC1 Device Control 1\x0a\x0a   STX Start of Text (CC)                  DC2 Device Control 2\x0a\x0a   ETX End of Text (CC)                    DC3 Device Control 3\x0a\x0a   EOT End of Transmission (CC)            DC4 Device Control 4 (Stop)\x0a\x0a   ENQ Enquiry (CC)                        NAK Negative Acknowledge (CC)\x0a\x0a   ACK Acknowledge (CC)                    SYN Synchronous Idle (CC)\x0a\x0a   BEL Bell (audible or                    ETB End of Transmission\x0a       attention signal)                       Block (CC)\x0a\x0a   BS Backspace (FE)                       CAN Cancel\x0a\x0a   HT Horizontal Tabulation                EM End of Medium\x0a      (punched card skip) (FE)\x0a\x0a   LF Line Feed (FE)                       SUB Substitute\x0a\x0a   VT Vertical Tabulation (FE)             ESC Escape\x0a\x0a   FF Form Feed (FE)                       FS File Separator IS)\x0a\x0a   CR Carriage Return (FE)                 GS Group Separator (IS)\x0a\x0a   SO Shift Out                            RS Record Separator (IS)\x0a\x0a   SI Shift In                             US Unit Separator (IS)\x0a\x0a                                           DEL Delete [1]\x0a\x0a   ________\x0a   NOTE: (CC) Communication Control\x0a         (FE) Format Effector\x0a         (IS) Information Separator\x0a   [1] In the strict sense, DEL is not a control character.  (See 5.2)\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 4]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-5\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h3\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-4.2\x22 href=\x22#section-4.2\x22>4.2</a> Graphic Characters</span>\x0a\x0a   Column/Row  Symbol      Name\x0a\x0a   2/0         SP          Space (Normally Non-Printing)\x0a   2/1         !           Exclamation Point\x0a   2/2         \x22           Quotation Marks (Diaeresis [2])\x0a   2/3         #           Number Sign [3,4]\x0a   2/4         $           Dollar Sign\x0a   2/5         %           Percent\x0a   2/6         &amp;           Ampersand\x0a   2/7         '           Apostrophe (Closing Single Quotation Mark\x0a                           Acute Accent [2])\x0a   2/8         (           Opening Parenthesis\x0a   2/9         )           Closing Parenthesis\x0a   2/10        *           Asterisk\x0a   2/11        +           Plus\x0a   2/12        ,           Comma (Cedilla [2])\x0a   2/13        -           Hyphen (Minus)\x0a   2/14        .           Period (Decimal Point)\x0a   2/15        /           Slant\x0a   3/10        :           Colon\x0a   3/11        ;           Semicolon\x0a   3/12        &lt;           Less Than\x0a   3/13        =           Equals\x0a   3/14        &gt;           Greater Than\x0a   3/15        ?           Question Mark\x0a   4/0         @           Commercial At [3]\x0a   5/11        [           Opening Bracket [3]\x0a   5/12       \x5c            Reverse Slant [3]\x0a   5/13        ]           Closing Bracket [3]\x0a   5/14        ^           Circumflex [2,3]\x0a   5/15        _           Underline\x0a   6/0         `           Grave Accent [2,3] (Opening Single Quotation\x0a                                   Mark)\x0a   7/11        {           Opening Brace [3]\x0a   7/12        |           Vertical Line [3]\x0a   7/13        }           Closing Brace [3]\x0a   7/14        ~           Overline [3] (Tilde [2]; General Accent [2])\x0a\x0a   ________\x0a      2 The use of the symbols in 2/2, 2/7, 2/12, 5/14, /6/0, and 7/14\x0a   as diacritical marks is described in <a href=\x22#appendix-A\x22>Appendix A</a>, A5.2\x0a      3 These characters should not be used in international interchange\x0a   without determining that there is agreement between sender and\x0a   recipient.  (See <a href=\x22#appendix-B\x22>Appendix B</a>4.)\x0a      4 In applications where there is no requirement for the symbol #,\x0a   the symbol (Pounds Sterling) may be used in position 2/3.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 5]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-6\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-5\x22 href=\x22#section-5\x22>5</a>. Definitions</span>\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h3\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-5.1\x22 href=\x22#section-5.1\x22>5.1</a> General</span>\x0a\x0a      (CC)  Communication Control: A functional character intended to\x0a   control or facilitate transmission of information over communication\x0a   networks.\x0a      (FE)  Format Effector: A functional character which controls the\x0a   layout or positioning of information in printing or display devices.\x0a      (IS) Information Separator: A character which is used to separate\x0a   and qualify information in a logical sense.  There is a group of four\x0a   such characters, which are to be used in a hierarchical order.\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h3\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-5.2\x22 href=\x22#section-5.2\x22>5.2</a> Control Characters</span>\x0a\x0a      NUL (Null): The all-zeros character which may serve to accomplish\x0a   time fill and media fill.\x0a      SOH (Start of Heading): A communication control character used at\x0a   the beginning of a sequence of characters which constitute a\x0a   machine-sensible address or routing information.  Such a sequence is\x0a   referred to as the \x22heading.\x22  An STX character has the effect of\x0a   terminating a heading.\x0a      STX (Start of Text): A communication control character which\x0a   precedes a sequence of characters that is to be treated as an entity\x0a   and entirely transmitted through to the ultimate destination.  Such a\x0a   sequence is referred to as \x22text.\x22  STX may be used to terminate a\x0a   sequence of characters started by SOH.\x0a      ETX (End of Text): A communication control character used to\x0a   terminate a sequence of characters started with STX and transmitted\x0a   as an entity.\x0a      EOT (End of Transmission): A communication control character used\x0a   to indicate the conclusion of a transmission, which may have\x0a   contained one or more texts and any associated headings.\x0a      ENQ (Enquiry): A communication control character used in data\x0a   communication systems as a request for a response from a remote\x0a   station.  It may be used as a \x22Who Are You\x22 (WRU) to obtain\x0a   identification, or may be used to obtain station status, or both.\x0a      ACK (Acknowledge): A communication control character transmitted\x0a   by a receiver as an affirmative response to a sender.\x0a      BEL (Bell): A character for use when there is a need to call for\x0a   human attention.  It may control alarm or attention devices.\x0a      BS (Backspace): A format effector which controls the movement of\x0a   the printing position one printing space backward on the same\x0a   printing line.  (Applicable also to display devices.)\x0a      HT (Horizontal Tabulation): A format effector which controls the\x0a   movement of the printing position to the next in a series of\x0a   predetermined positions along the printing line.  (Applicable also to\x0a   display devices and the skip function on punched cards.)\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 6]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-7\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a      LF (Line Feed): A format effector which controls the movement of\x0a   the printing position to the next printing line.  (Applicable also to\x0a   display devices.) Where appropriate, this character may have the\x0a   meaning \x22New Line\x22 (NL), a format effector which controls the\x0a   movement of the printing point to the first printing position on the\x0a   next printing line.  Use of this convention requires agreement\x0a   between sender and recipient of data.\x0a      VT (Vertical Tabulation): A format effector which controls the\x0a   movement of the printing position to the next in a series of\x0a   predetermined printing lines.  (Applicable also to display devices.)\x0a      FF (Form Feed): A format effector which controls the movement of\x0a   the printing position to the first pre-determined printing line on\x0a   the next form or page.  (Applicable also to display devices.)\x0a      CR (Carriage Return): A format effector which controls the\x0a   movement of the printing position to the first printing position on\x0a   the same printing line.  (Applicable also to display devices.)\x0a      SO (Shift Out): A control character indicating that the code\x0a   combinations which follow shall be interpreted as outside of the\x0a   character set of the standard code table until a Shift In character\x0a   is reached.\x0a      SI (Shift In): A control character indicating that the code\x0a   combinations which follow shall be interpreted according to the\x0a   standard code table.\x0a      DLE (Data Link Escape): A communication control character which\x0a   will change the meaning of a limited number of contiguously following\x0a   characters.  It is used exclusively to provide supplementary controls\x0a   in data communication networks.\x0a      DC1, DC2, DC3, DC4 (Device Controls): Characters for the control\x0a   of ancillary devices associated with data processing or\x0a   telecommunication systems, more especially switching devices \x22on\x22 or\x0a   \x22off.\x22  (If a single \x22stop\x22 control is required to interrupt or turn\x0a   off ancillary devices, DC4 is the preferred assignment.)\x0a      NAK (Negative Acknowledge): A communication control character\x0a   transmitted by a receiver as a negative response to the sender.\x0a      SYN (Synchronous Idle): A communication control character used by\x0a   a synchronous transmission system in the absence of any other\x0a   character to provide a signal from which synchronism may be achieved\x0a   or retained.\x0a      ETB (End of Transmission Block): A communication control character\x0a   used to indicate the end of a block of data for communication\x0a   purposes.  ETB is used for blocking data where the block structure is\x0a   not necessarily related to the processing format.\x0a      CAN (Cancel): A control character used to indicate that the data\x0a   with which it is sent is in error or is to be disregarded.\x0a      EM (End of Medium): A control character associated with the sent\x0a   data which may be used to identify the physical end of the medium, or\x0a   the end of the used, or wanted, portion of information recorded on a\x0a   medium.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 7]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-8\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a(The position of this character does not necessarily correspond to the\x0a   physical end of the medium.)\x0a\x0a      SUB (Substitute): A character that may be substituted for a\x0a   character which is determined to be invalid or in error.\x0a      ESC (Escape): A control character intended to provide code\x0a   extension (supplementary characters) in general information\x0a   interchange.  The Escape character itself is a prefix affecting the\x0a   interpretation of a limited number of contiguously following\x0a   characters.\x0a      FS (File Separator), GS (Group Separator), RS (Record Separator),\x0a   and US (Unit Separator): These information separators may be used\x0a   within data in optional fashion, except that their hierarchical\x0a   relationship shall be: FS is the most inclusive, then GS, then RS,\x0a   and US is least inclusive.  (The content and length of a File, Group,\x0a   Record, or Unit are not specified.)\x0a      DEL (Delete): This character is used primarily to \x22erase\x22 or\x0a   \x22obliterate\x22 erroneous or unwanted characters in perforated tape.\x0a   (In the strict sense, DEL is not a control character.)\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h3\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-5.3\x22 href=\x22#section-5.3\x22>5.3</a> Graphic Characters</span>\x0a\x0a      SP (Space): A normally non-printing graphic character used to\x0a   separate words.  It is also a format effector which controls the\x0a   movement of the printing position, one printing position forward.\x0a   (Applicable also to display devices.)\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22h2\x22><a class=\x22selflink\x22 id=\x22section-6\x22 href=\x22#section-6\x22>6</a>. General Considerations</span>\x0a\x0a   6.1 This standard does not define the means by which the coded set is\x0a   to be recorded in any physical medium, nor does it include any\x0a   redundancy or define techniques for error control.  Further, this\x0a   standard does not define data communication character structure, data\x0a   communication formats, code extension techniques, or graphic\x0a   representation of control characters.\x0a\x0a   6.2 Deviations from the standard may create serious difficulties in\x0a   information interchange and should be used only with full cognizance\x0a   of the parties involved.\x0a\x0a   6.3 The relative sequence of any two characters, when used as a basis\x0a   for collation, is defined by their binary values.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22>Cert                                                            [Page 8]</span></pre>\x0a<hr class='noprint'/><!--NewPage--><pre class='newpage'><span id=\x22page-9\x22 ></span>\x0a<span class=\x22grey\x22><a href=\x22./rfc20\x22>RFC 20</a>            ASCII format for Network Interchange      October 1969</span>\x0a\x0a\x0a   6.4 No specific meaning is prescribed for any of the graphics in the\x0a   code table except that which is understood by the users.\x0a   Furthermore, this standard does not specify a type style for the\x0a   printing or display of the various graphic characters.  In specific\x0a   applications, it may be desirable to employ distinctive styling of\x0a   individual graphics to facilitate their use for specific purposes as,\x0a   for example, to stylize the graphics in code positions 2/1 and 5/15\x0a   into those frequently associated with logical OR (|) and logical NOT\x0a   (252), respectively.\x0a\x0a   6.5 The appendixes to this standard contain additional information on\x0a   the design and use of this code.\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a         [ This RFC was put into machine readable form for entry ]\x0a           [ into the online RFC archives by Robbie Bennet 9/99]\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0a\x0aCert                                                            [Page 9]\x0a</pre>\x0a")
